  "summary_text": "Kyle, Port Washington is running slightly behind calendar-adjusted expectations for the month\u2014down 6.6% against DOW-adjusted baseline\u2014but the core signal is operational capacity, not demand softness. Yesterday with only one groomer on the floor, revenue per groomer hit $385, well below the 30-day baseline of $474; today you've got five scheduled, which should recover that productivity gap. The bright spot: core grooming revenue is up 11% year-over-week, add-on attach improved to 78%, and your year-to-date growth of 36% revenue and 35% appointments shows structural strength. Retail softness (down 22% yesterday) warrants attention\u2014this is a front-of-house discipline issue, not a demand issue, and should tighten.\n\nHicksville is performing. Month-to-date, you're actually *ahead* of DOW-adjusted expectation by 7.5%, up 12% year-over-year with 14% more appointments. Yesterday's single-groomer shift still produced $475/groomer, 15% above the 30-day baseline of $414. The add-on attach at 40% is lower than Port Washington's 78%\u2014flag that for the team to upsell more consistently. Hicksville's groomer productivity ($424/groomer-day MTD) is improving relative to September ($381), a genuine operational win.\n\nOne pattern across both locations: Port Washington is capacity-constrained relative to demand, while Hicksville is operating leaner but hotter. Your Port lapse pool of 516 customers ($318K annual value) and Hicksville's 193 ($139K) represent reactivation opportunity\u2014focus there before adding acquisition spend. Inventory gaps (bullies, treat tables) are across both locations; reorder immediately.\n\n**Capacity & coverage: Port Washington has 5 groomer-days today and 15 bather hours; Hicksville has 1 groomer-day today and 0 bathers; next 7 days: Port 36 groomer-days and 83 bather hours, Hicksville 22 groomer-days and 0 bathers\u2014Hicksville needs bather reinforcement.**"
